===== [143/150]  What's the difference between the Fine Art and Digital Art tracks? =====

Q: What's the difference between the Fine Art and Digital Art tracks?

retrieved: digital-art=0.508, fine-art-kids=0.502, fine-art-teen=0.480, programs-overview=0.476

A: Fine Art focuses on traditional media and broad art skills: painting, drawing, sculpture, mixed media, printmaking, installation, art history, museum trips, exhibitions, and competitions. It’s organized by age/skill (Art Exploration, Art Foundation, Art Advancement, Teen Sketchbook/Drawing/Painting/Portfolio) and trains fundamentals through observational work and portfolio development for AP/college.

Digital Art focuses on digital illustration, painting, and animation using tablets and professional software. Classes teach cartoon/illustration, typography, visual development, infographic/graphic design, digital painting, anime/comics, and animation tools. Digital classes require a device and include electives like Digital Anime and Animation Lab; some courses emphasize portfolio work and professional digital painting.

In short: Fine Art emphasizes hands-on, traditional media and art history/real-world studio practices; Digital Art emphasizes tablet-based, software-driven illustration/animation and digital portfolio skills.

===== [144/150]  How do I know if my child is ready to move from Exploration to the next level? =====

Q: How do I know if my child is ready to move from Exploration to the next level?

retrieved: class-levels=0.490, faq-potty-trained=0.389, fine-art-kids=0.359, free-trial=0.342

A: Students move up based on age, demonstrated skills, completion of the current level, and the instructor’s evaluation. For the Kids Fine Art pathway, Art Exploration is for ages 4–6; to advance you and the instructor will consider:

- Age: the next level (Art Foundation) is typically for ages 7–9.  
- Completion and proficiency: successful completion of Exploration and demonstrated proficiency in the skills/techniques taught.  
- Instructor evaluation: the teacher assesses progress from class performance, any homework/assignments, and readiness to handle more advanced projects.

If your child is on the age boundary and has little experience, start at the lower level; the teacher will recommend promotion when ready. If you want personalized guidance, contact any CalColor staff for help.

